    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0450" n="410" xml:id="pg410"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.74/10–2551: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d180-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">niact</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-10-25T15:00:00+02:00">October 25, 1951—3
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>568. Stevenson was in despair last night. Notwithstanding Gen Robertson’s promise
                    to him in the morning he was threatening last night to turn off tap again
                    because he was not sure “Egypts had learned their lesson”. I took high exception
                    to this and Stevenson promised to do his best with Brit mil. “Remember,
                    however,” he remarked, “We in England are without a govt now”.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d180-f2"/> In addition to formidable list consequence set out in
                    mytel 55 [<hi rendition="#italic">555</hi>]<anchor n="3" xml:id="d180-f3"/> I
                    should add important irrigation pumps and domestic cooking. Furthermore,
                    bakeries have fuel oil on hand for about three days. What wld mobs do in dark
                    streets?</p>
                <p>Brit Shell manager says cessation fuel oil traffic out of Suez wld force refinery
                    to close within two or three days owing lack storage facilities. This wld immed
                    force shut-down producing fields and deny white refinery products for pipeline
                    transport. Supplies thus lost wld be irremedial inasmuch as transport capacity
                    normally worked at maximum.</p>
                <p>Brit mil significantly have informed Stevenson that their position in Canal Zone
                    is untenable unless they receive minimum coop from Egypts. I retorted, “That is
                    not the way to get it”.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d180-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d180-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d180-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to London, Paris for MacArthur, and Ankara.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f2" n="2" xml:id="d180-f2-content">
                <p>General elections were being held throughout the United Kingdom on this day which
                    led to a change of governments, the Conservative Party led by Winston Churchill
                    replacing the Labour Party of Clement Attlee.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f3" n="3" xml:id="d180-f3-content">
                <p>Dated October 23, <ref target="#pg407">p. 407</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f4" n="4" xml:id="d180-f4-content">
                <p>In telegram 569 from Cairo, October 25, Caffery reported that Stevenson was able
                    to persuade the British military not to turn off the oil taps again “and he is
                    proceeding to Suez tomorrow to talk to Robertson and endeavor to persuade him
                    that decision of that category shld be on cabinet level.” Caffery added that
                    Stevenson was cabling London “to put whatever govt comes in as result of
                    elections on notice that very grave decisions must be taken soon at London.”
                    (641.74/10–2551)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
